Overload C functions on argument type with gnu::overloadable

Clang has an "overloadable" attribute that lets several C functions
share a name, chosen between by argument type, with C++ name mangling
applied so that the symbols stay distinct.  GCC rejected the idea
upstream.  This adds the same feature to the C front end, spelled
__attribute__((overloadable)) or [[gnu::overloadable]].

Overload sets need no new data structure.  bind already keeps a name's
bindings in a chain sorted by scope depth, and pop_scope unwinds several
bindings of one name in one scope correctly, so pushdecl only has to
decide whether a new declaration redeclares an existing binding or joins
it.  Two declarations name the same function when their parameter lists
name the same types; lists that are merely compatible, such as an enum
and the integer type representing it, mangle differently and so
overload.  A call is resolved in c_build_function_call_vec, beside the
type-directed overloading GCC already does for the __atomic builtins,
and before convert_arguments, which rewrites the arguments that the
ranking needs.

Mangling is a new C-subset Itanium writer.  cp/mangle.cc cannot be
reused: it is a cc1plus object that needs cp-tree.h and the C++ garbage
collection roots, and TYPE_LANG_SLOT_1 is TYPE_RAISES_EXCEPTIONS there
but TYPE_ACTUAL_ARG_TYPES in the C front end, so it would silently
mangle C function types wrong.  The output here was checked against g++
for every type C can express, substitution compression included.

Two deliberate differences from Clang, whose documentation does not
describe its own behavior accurately anyway.  An overloaded name is
resolved only by a call written directly on it, so taking its address,
or parenthesizing it as in (f) (x), is an error unless the set has a
single member; build_external_ref learns whether a name is being called
from the parser peeking for an open paren, and nothing later in the
front end can tell an unresolved overload from a chosen one.  And
_Atomic is mangled as an extended qualifier written outside the
cv-qualifiers, following the ABI grammar and GCC's own C++ front end,
where Clang writes it inside them.

