+@atindex @code{overloadable}
+@cindex functions overloaded on their argument types
+@item overloadable
+This attribute, a GNU extension to C modelled on the Clang attribute of
+the same name, lets several functions share one name and be told apart
+by the types of their arguments:
+
+@smallexample
+float __attribute__ ((overloadable)) tgsin (float x) @{ return sinf (x); @}
+double __attribute__ ((overloadable)) tgsin (double x) @{ return sin (x); @}
+long double __attribute__ ((overloadable)) tgsin (long double x)
+  @{ return sinl (x); @}
+@end smallexample
+
+A call picks the member of the set whose parameters need the least
+conversion, following the C++ rules with the adjustments C needs: a
+conversion between compatible but distinct types, such as an enumerated
+type and the integer type it is compatible with, ranks as a conversion
+rather than as an exact match, and widening from @code{float} ranks as a
+promotion whether the destination is @code{double} or @code{long
+double}.  A call that fits no member, or fits two equally well, is an
+error.
+
+Because the members share a source name, they are given distinct
+assembler names using the same Itanium ABI mangling C++ uses, so
+@code{tgsin (1.0f)} above calls @code{_Z5tgsinf}.  Do not depend on a
+particular mangling; declaring these functions @code{static inline} is
+the safest use.  At most one member of a set may omit the attribute, for
+compatibility with an existing unmangled function; that one keeps its
+ordinary assembler name.  An explicit @code{asm} label still overrides
+the mangled name.
+
+Functions with this attribute must have a prototype, though as in C++ a
+prototype consisting only of an ellipsis is allowed.  Two declarations
+of the name whose parameter lists are compatible are declarations of the
+same function, so they must agree about the attribute, and a difference
+only in the return type is a conflict rather than an overload.
+
+The attribute is accepted in C++, where it does nothing: functions there
+already overload, and already use this mangling.
+
+This implementation differs from Clang's in two ways worth noting.
+First, an overloaded name is resolved only by a call written directly on
+it.  Using the name anywhere else, including taking its address or
+parenthesizing it as in @code{(f) (x)}, is an error unless the set has a
+single member; adding an overload to an existing function therefore
+breaks code that takes that function's address.  Second, @code{_Atomic}
+is mangled as an extended qualifier written outside the
+@code{const}/@code{volatile} qualifiers, following the ABI grammar and
+the C++ front end, where Clang writes it inside them.
+
